<main><section class="page-intro"><div class="wrap page-intro-inner"><p class="eyebrow">Research dossier</p><h1 class="page-title">Georgina Lloyd Taylor</h1><p class="page-deck">Work, field research and the diary</p><p class="meta">Edgardo Civallero · 13 October 2026</p></div></section><section class="page-reading"><div class="wrap page-reading-grid"><aside class="page-toc"><p class="page-toc-title">On this page</p><nav><a href="#investigation">A diary of work and dependence</a><a href="#diary">The notebook, its author and its edition</a><a href="#specimens">Collecting and keeping specimens</a><a href="#camp">Camp life and local support</a><a href="#perspective">Reading Georgina critically</a><a href="#connections">Following the documentary connections</a><a href="#sources">Sources, references and use</a></nav></aside><article class="page-prose"><section id="investigation" class="prose-section">
<h2>A diary of work and dependence</h2>
<p>Rosamond Georgina Lloyd Taylor travelled to the Galápagos Islands with her husband, Thomas, as a participant in the 1938–1939 Lack–Venables expedition. Her diary follows their journey from Britain to Ecuador and the islands, and their return through Panama, between December 1938 and April 1939. Alongside encounters with island wildlife, she recorded collecting plants, preparing specimens, cooking, obtaining supplies and living with her companions. Her account offers a detailed view of the everyday circumstances in which field research took place.</p>
<p>This dossier is based on <em>Galapagos Diary</em>, the English edition published by the Charles Darwin Foundation in 2020, with transcription, editing and notes by Edgardo Civallero. The edition makes the handwritten diary available as an annotated text and includes an introduction explaining the document’s history and the identification of its author. The discussion below draws on that published edition; references identify its printed page numbers.</p>
<p><a class="text-link" href="https://www.darwinfoundation.org/en/resources/publications-of-the-library-archive/galapagos-diary/">Read the 2020 edition at the Charles Darwin Foundation</a>.</p>
<p>One entry brings the different kinds of work together particularly clearly. On 3 February 1939, Georgina and Thomas spent part of the morning collecting plants along the shore of Pelican Cove on Santa Cruz. After lunch, she pressed the plants while he worked on flower pigments. The day ended with singing in the tent and conversation outside. Collecting, preparing specimens and spending an evening together appear within the same account of a day’s activities (pp. 52–53).</p>
<p>Following these activities through the diary helps us understand Georgina’s participation in the expedition and the practical demands of research. Plants needed to be dried and protected from mould; food and equipment had to be obtained and maintained; residents provided water, accommodation and assistance. Scientific work took place within a landscape already inhabited and used by other people, whose contributions also enter her account.</p>
<p>The diary preserves Georgina’s perspective on those experiences, including her pleasures, frustrations and judgments. Reading it closely means examining both the activities she describes and the ways she represents her companions and island residents. This dossier brings selected passages together to explore that relationship between field research, everyday work and the people who made the expedition possible.</p>
</section><section id="diary" class="prose-section">
<h2>The notebook, its author and its edition</h2>
<p>The diary survives in a cloth-bound commercial notebook measuring approximately 20 × 13 centimetres. According to the introduction to the 2020 edition, it contains 158 pages, of which 124 are handwritten. Its entries run from 15 December 1938 to 29 April 1939. Although the cover bears a title and almost-erased initials, the narrative does not explicitly name its author.</p>
<p>Identifying the writer required information beyond the diary itself. The edition’s introduction describes accompanying notes that record how her identity was established. In November 1984, Jan Castle and Chris McFarling examined references within the account and identified its author as Rosamond Georgina Lloyd Taylor. A note written by G. T. Corley Smith on 24 April 1986 confirmed the identification and stated that she had given him the diary for research use at the Charles Darwin Research Station. John Simcox supplied her full name in another note, dated 2 September 2002.</p>
<p>These notes help explain how the notebook became an identifiable historical source. Its text preserved an account of the expedition, while later readers supplied information connecting that account to a particular person. The edition does not establish when Georgina donated the diary. Corley Smith also reported that a covering letter and other papers had been lost, leaving gaps in the surviving account of its transfer.</p>
<p>The published text reflects a further stage in the document’s history. In preparing the 2020 edition, Civallero regularized spelling and formatting, expanded abbreviations, omitted crossed-out words and incorporated additions written between the lines. Those decisions made the diary easier to read, while shaping the form in which readers encounter it today.</p>
<p>The distinction matters when using the edition as evidence. Georgina’s entries describe the journey and her experiences; Civallero’s introduction and notes explain the manuscript, its history and aspects of its content. This dossier draws on both, identifying which part of the edition supports each discussion. The notebook’s physical description, the identification notes and the editorial procedures summarized here are described in the introduction (pp. 8–14).</p>
</section><section id="specimens" class="prose-section">
<h2>Collecting and keeping specimens</h2>
<p>During their time in the Galápagos, Georgina and Thomas collected plants on excursions along the coast and elsewhere in the islands. Georgina’s entries describe both gathering the plants and preparing the material afterwards: pressing it, changing drying papers and packing it for travel. Following those entries reveals the daily work involved in keeping a botanical collection in usable condition.</p>
<p>Pressing plants flattens the material between sheets of paper so that it can be dried and preserved for study. The paper absorbs moisture and needs to be changed as drying proceeds. Georgina’s account shows her taking part in this preparation. On 3 February, after collecting with Thomas along the shore of Pelican Cove on Santa Cruz, she pressed their plants while he worked on flower pigments. Later entries describe changing drying papers and preparing the collection for transport (3 February, pp. 52–53; 20–22 February, pp. 67–69; 15–16 March, p. 83).</p>
<p>Keeping the material dry became a recurring difficulty. On 11 February, Georgina reported mould on specimens. Attempts to dry them the following day were unsuccessful, and by 18 February both the plants and their drying papers were deteriorating. Without sufficient sunshine or wind, moisture remained a threat to the collection (11–18 February, pp. 61–66). These entries show how the work of gathering plants continued afterwards through repeated efforts to preserve them.</p>
<p>A visit to Genovesa in April brought similar difficulties. On 3 April, Georgina and Thomas collected plants and sheltered the portfolio holding them during heavy rain. Three days later, she described drying the material aboard ship. She reported approximately 28 collected plants and stated that several had not previously been recorded from the island (3–6 April, pp. 101–104). Her entry conveys the importance she attached to their collecting. Establishing which plants represented new island records would require comparison with botanical records and the specimens themselves.</p>
<p>The couple also collected animals. On 11 March, Georgina recorded catching yellow butterflies with Thomas for someone she identified as Professor Carpenter. On 27 March, she packed and labelled insects and placed a scorpion in alcohol (11 March, p. 81; 27 March, p. 95). Labelling kept information with the collected material, while alcohol provided a different means of preserving an animal specimen. Her participation therefore extended to handling and preparing several kinds of collection.</p>
<p>Across these passages, Georgina appears as an active participant in collecting and specimen preparation. The diary records particular actions rather than a complete allocation of responsibilities between her and Thomas. Its detail nevertheless allows us to follow work that could otherwise disappear behind a brief collecting credit: changing damp papers, protecting material from rain, watching for mould and preparing specimens for the journey onward.</p>
</section><section id="camp" class="prose-section">
<h2>Camp life and local support</h2>
<p>While staying at Academy Bay on Santa Cruz, the expedition depended on island residents for practical assistance. Georgina records that Mrs. Kübler allowed the visitors to leave their belongings on her property, pitch their tents in the grounds and obtain drinking water. Subsequent entries describe gifts of food, shared meals, accommodation and help with travel. These encounters place the expedition within an inhabited island community whose resources and hospitality helped sustain its stay (31 January–6 February, pp. 49–55).</p>
<p>Life in camp also required daily work from the visitors themselves. Georgina describes cooking, repairing equipment, managing supplies and caring for companions. On 22 February, she recorded cooking alongside drying plants while another expedition member was ill. Two days later, disagreements over rations led to a division of supplies between the Taylors and the other men. Obtaining and distributing food affected both everyday relations and the practical arrangements for continuing their work (22–24 February, pp. 68–71).</p>
<p>Responsibility for these tasks extended beyond Georgina. Richard Leacock, another expedition participant, cooked and repaired machinery. Residents helped the visitors find their way and provided places to stay, while boats and donkeys carried supplies. On 9 March, Georgina recorded the arrival of a donkey-load of food delivered by a man she did not name (24–28 February, pp. 70–74; 9 March, p. 80). The entry preserves his contribution, although it gives readers little opportunity to learn about the person who made it.</p>
<p>Such details help explain how field research was sustained from day to day. A collecting excursion required provisions; a camp needed water; equipment needed repair; illness changed what companions could do. Georgina’s diary brings these demands into the same account as observations of plants and animals. It also shows that the expedition’s practical support involved people whose lives and activities extended beyond the visitors’ research.</p>
<p>Food supplies included island wildlife, and Georgina’s account records contrasting responses to its use. On 2 March, she described processing turtle meat and oil. In the same entry, she objected to the killing of rare tortoises when tortoise liver arrived at camp (pp. 75–76). Her participation in preparing one animal for use and her objection to the killing of another reveal distinctions within her own attitudes towards wildlife.</p>
<p>These episodes give camp life a place in the history of the expedition. Meals, supplies, repairs, hospitality and disagreements shaped the circumstances in which collecting and observation took place. Through Georgina’s account, we can follow some of those relationships while also noticing how unevenly the people involved are identified and described.</p>
</section><section id="perspective" class="prose-section">
<h2>Reading Georgina critically</h2>
<p>Georgina’s diary records the expedition through her own experiences. She describes boredom, exhaustion, enjoyment and disagreements alongside collecting and travel. Those personal responses help readers understand how the journey felt to one participant. They also shape her descriptions of the people she encountered and the places she visited.</p>
<p>Her account includes racist language and judgments about class and civilization in descriptions of some crew members and island residents. These passages reveal prejudices that influenced how she represented other people. When using the diary to reconstruct an encounter, we therefore need to distinguish the actions she reports from the judgments she attaches to them. A person who supplied food or assisted the expedition may appear chiefly through a dismissive description, sometimes without even being named.</p>
<p>This uneven treatment affects what we can learn from the narrative. Georgina gives readers considerable access to her own thoughts, while the residents and workers she describes rarely have an opportunity to explain their actions or express their views. Their contributions remain important to the expedition’s history, even where her account provides only a fragment of their involvement.</p>
<p>Her observations of wildlife require a different kind of attention. On Española, she described small, colourful reptiles as land iguanas; an editorial note in the 2020 edition proposes that they were lava lizards. On 23 March, she reported shallow burrows and inferred that iguanas laid eggs in them (30 January, p. 49; 23 March, pp. 90–91). These examples distinguish what she saw, how she understood it and how the editor later interpreted her description. Keeping those stages visible allows readers to follow the reasoning behind an identification.</p>
<p>The same care applies to less definite descriptions. Her comparison of certain trees to an “Ilex type” conveys a resemblance; it does not necessarily mean that she identified them as belonging to the genus <em>Ilex</em> (p. 51). Turning an approximate comparison into a firm botanical identification would give her statement a certainty it did not originally possess.</p>
<p>Reading the diary critically means retaining this variety of perspectives and levels of certainty. Georgina could participate in collecting, struggle with daily work, enjoy her surroundings and express prejudices within the same narrative. Attending to those features helps us understand both what her account preserves and how her position within the expedition shaped the record.</p>
</section><section id="connections" class="prose-section">
<h2>Following the documentary connections</h2>
<p>The diary brings several aspects of the expedition into view together. Georgina’s collecting appears alongside the effort to preserve specimens; excursions depend on provisions and transport; camp life involves residents as well as visiting researchers. Following these connections helps us understand how particular activities fitted into the expedition’s daily circumstances.</p>
<p>The notebook’s later history adds another dimension. Georgina recorded the journey, subsequent readers documented her identity, and the 2020 edition made the text publicly accessible. The account available today therefore carries both her narrative and information supplied by people who preserved, identified and edited it. Each contribution helps readers understand a different part of the document’s history.</p>
<p>Other connections remain to be investigated. Specimen labels and collection registers could help establish whether surviving plants or insects correspond to the collecting events Georgina described. Expedition correspondence and publications could offer other accounts of shared activities. Sources concerning island residents could provide fuller histories of people who appear only briefly in her narrative.</p>
<p>Such comparisons would allow us to follow particular events beyond the diary. A record of collecting might be connected to a specimen and its subsequent study; a passing reference to local assistance might become part of a named person’s history. These possibilities arise from specific passages, although the connections themselves still require evidence.</p>
<p>Within Galapagensis, these questions contribute to research on the <a href="/research-agenda/#line-1">Distributed Galápagos Archive</a>, <a href="/research-agenda/#line-2">People Missing from the Record</a> and <a href="/research-agenda/#line-5">Science in the Islands</a>. The diary provides a starting point for tracing how people, documents and collections became connected, and how those relationships can be understood today.</p>
